{"description":"GitHub issue maleolabs/eka-cli#28 (bug, medium, source: agent, EKA 1.2.0): the eka_new MCP tool intermittently refuses with 'new requires {project, namespace, type, id}' although all required params are present; a byte-identical retry succeeds. Failing calls carried ~2-4KB unicode-heavy content payloads.\n\nRevision 2 (2026-08-26, planning-time amendment) - CONTRACT DRIFT CONFIRMED, DIRECTION REVERSED: the live deployed MCP surface enforces ALL FOUR identity fields (project, namespace, type, id) as required parameters - matching the reporter's refusal message verbatim. The expansion-branch source reviewed in v1 enforces and advertises only {type,id}. So the reviewed SOURCE lags the DEPLOYED build, not the other way around; the v1 'binary differs from repo source' observation stands with the direction corrected. This STRENGTHENS hypothesis H2: calls omitting project/namespace - which a model may skip when its schema knowledge is stale or when large content crowds generation - are refused deterministically while the model believes all required params were present. H1 (client-side truncation/framing of large multibyte payloads breaking the stdio line) remains the best explanation for the size-correlated intermittency, to be discriminated by refusal-time diagnostics once shipped.\n\nUnchanged verified facts:\n- The refusal is emitted at ONE condition in internal/mcp/server.go callTool case \"new\": json.Unmarshal(args, \u0026p) error OR empty required string field - one schema-shaped message conflating THREE distinct causes: (1) malformed/truncated JSON arguments, (2) missing field, (3) present-but-empty or wrong-typed field.\n- The MCP server request path is deterministic and single-goroutine (newline-delimited stdio transport, generous line cap, correct reassembly, fuzz-hardened, no shared mutable state). A byte-identical retry succeeding PROVES the server received different bytes the first time - nondeterminism lives upstream of HandleMessage (client/transport side).\n- Go's encoding/json does not reject unicode/lone surrogates; unicode per se cannot trip the parser - byte length is the plausible discriminator.\n\nFix direction (sharpened): make the refusal accurate (per-cause messages); define the required-field contract ONCE and derive both inputSchema and runtime validation from it so drift cannot recur; log argument byte-length at refusal time for diagnostics; reconcile release/version tagging so deployed builds are identifiable (local repo build reports 'dev' while the deployed plugin is a newer generation). Do NOT chase a unicode parsing fix - the decoder does not reject unicode.","impact":"Erodes the core contract that agent workflows depend on: EKA refusals are treated by agents as deterministic and accurate, so a refusal that claims required params are absent when they were sent causes wasted retry round-trips, trains agents to distrust or work around refusal messages, and - worst case - leads an agent to 'fix' a non-problem (dropping fields, shrinking payloads) based on a misreported cause. The schema/enforcement drift behind it means every client sees a different contract depending on which build it talks to.","acceptanceCriteria":"- Refusal at the eka_new tool entry discriminates its three causes: JSON parse failure surfaces the sanitized decoder error; missing field names WHICH field is absent; empty/wrong-typed field names the field.\n- Required-field contract defined ONCE and derived into BOTH the advertised inputSchema and the runtime validation, so drift between them cannot recur; a test asserts schema == enforcement.\n- Argument byte-length logged (stderr diagnostics) on every identity-shaped refusal, to discriminate client-side truncation from param omission.\n- Regression tests cover all three refusal causes plus a large (~4KB) unicode-heavy content payload.\n- Root cause of the intermittency identified and documented (client-side truncation vs omitted params vs double-encoded args); deployed binary reconciled with repo source (4-field vs 2-field enforcement discrepancy explained) and release versioning aligned so builds are identifiable.\n- Review findings posted as a comment on maleolabs/eka-cli#28; issue closed only after the fix ships in a release."}